{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 33, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "*highlighted section part 1*  \nRaymond III was the Count of Tripoli from 1152 until his death in 1187. He was born in 1140 and was the son of Raymond II and Hodierna of Jerusalem. His early life was shaped by the turbulent politics of the Crusader states. He inherited the county of Tripoli at the age of twelve after his father was assassinated. Due to his young age a regency was established with his mother Hodierna and his uncle Joscelin II of Edessa managing affairs until he came of age. Raymond III was a key figure in the Kingdom of Jerusalem and held considerable influence in the politics of the Levant during a period of intense conflict with Muslim powers.\n\n*highlighted section part 2*  \nRaymond III became a close advisor to King Baldwin IV of Jerusalem who suffered from leprosy. As Baldwin’s condition worsened Raymond was appointed regent of the kingdom in 1174 and again in 1183. He was known for his cautious diplomacy and often favored negotiation over military confrontation. This approach brought him into conflict with more aggressive nobles like Guy of Lusignan and Reynald of Châtillon. Raymond was also a key player in the succession crisis that followed Baldwin IV’s death. He supported the claim of Baldwin V the young son of Sibylla and William of Montferrat. His efforts to secure a stable succession were undermined by factional infighting among the nobility.\n\n*highlighted section part 3*  \nRaymond III played a central role in the events leading to the Battle of Hattin in 1187. He was one of the few nobles who warned against engaging Saladin in open battle. He advocated for defensive strategies and the preservation of Christian strongholds. His warnings were ignored and the Crusader army suffered a devastating defeat. After the battle Raymond returned to Tripoli and prepared for the inevitable Muslim assault. He died in 1187 shortly after the fall of Jerusalem. His death marked the end of a long and influential line of counts of Tripoli. Raymond III is remembered as a prudent and experienced leader who struggled to maintain unity among the fractious Crusader nobility. His legacy is one of diplomacy in a time of war and of a noble who sought peace amid relentless conflict.", "logprobs": [{"token": "*", "logprob": -3.111314072157256e-05}, {"token": "highlight", "logprob": -0.0024852838832885027}, {"token": "ed", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " section", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " part", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -1.1920928244535389e-07}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "*", "logprob": -3.71926071238704e-05}, {"token": "  \n", "logprob": -0.03804221749305725}, {"token": "Ray", "logprob": -7.152555099310121e-07}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -8.344646857949556e-07}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -9.417489309271332e-06}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.008450583554804325}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.014097276143729687}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.09045735746622086}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -1.1920928244535389e-07}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": -1.0609570381348021e-05}, {"token": "oli", "logprob": -3.576278118089249e-07}, {"token": " from", "logprob": -0.0009839460253715515}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.00025376438861712813}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -5.960462772236497e-07}, {"token": "5", "logprob": -3.135155202471651e-05}, {"token": "2", "logprob": -1.1920922133867862e-06}, {"token": " until", "logprob": -0.3132786452770233}, {"token": " his", "logprob": -0.0022558735217899084}, {"token": " death", "logprob": -2.7418097943154862e-06}, {"token": " in", "logprob": -1.9550132492440753e-05}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -1.7881377516459906e-06}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "8", "logprob": -2.264974000354414e-06}, {"token": "7", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": ".", "logprob": -0.00114970